We had a similar drift problem.  After implementing warm starting ( and contact point caching ) the problem went away.  <br><br>So you need to keep track of contact points that are the same between frames, and store the constraint force applied at the previous frame.  Start your solution vector with those values.  <br><br>This worked perfectly when no penetration recovery was employed.  I had to mess around a bit to get our non-standard penetration recovery to behave properly without upsetting the stack. So make sure your stack exhibits no horizontal drift when you disable penetration recovery( "baumgart" or "post-whatever-stabilization", I think are the terms in use ) first.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=323">mewert</a> — Mon Jun 05, 2006 11:10 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
