		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=1057#p1057"><![CDATA[
This is a very difficult to say from the information you are providing. <br><br>- How many contact points do you generate? <br>- Do you use SAT or GJK with some kind of manifold like Erwin? <br>- How do you choose the tangential direction at the contact point / manifold center?  <br>- How many friction constraints do you define per point - two?<br>- Do you solve for the friction at every contact point or at the center of the manifold like suggested by A. Moravanzky in GPG 4 or Erin Catto at the GDC?<br>- What is the gravity?<br>- What is the timestep? Is it constant or fluctuating?<br>- How many iterations?<br><br>Do you have any validation code that measures the constraint error ( that is the relative velocity in direction of the tangents) after running the PGS solver and applying the correcting impulses / forces?<br><br>As Erin said in another thread here. You really should make sure that two boxes (1x1x1) with 1kg mass are stable at a gravity of 10 with a constant timestep of 60 Hz at about 10 iterations. This should be stable even without warmstarting from my experience. If this doesn't work it is very likely that you are doing something wrong in either your constraint setup or solving.  The best way to validate your solver later is a simple stack of about 5 boxes like e.g. the two stack demos in Erin Cattos 2D engine from the GDC this year...<br><br>-Dirk<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=14">Dirk Gregorius</a> — Tue May 23, 2006 9:00 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
